{% from "docs-example.njk" import example %}
<h1 id="grid">Grid</h1>
<p>Use CSS Grid for rows and columns. Add <code>grid</code> for display, a column utility for the tracks, and a gap utility for spacing.</p>
<h2 id="fractional-tracks">Selected fractional tracks</h2>
<p>Choose a track pattern and a breakpoint explicitly. Each pattern changes columns at its minimum viewport width; combine it with <code>grid</code> to enable grid display. Below that breakpoint, these examples stack in source order.</p>
{% set markup %}<div class="grid grid-md-1fr-1fr grid-lg-1fr-1fr-1fr gap-16">
  <div class="docs-demo-item">One</div>
  <div class="docs-demo-item">Two</div>
  <div class="docs-demo-item">Three</div>
</div>{% endset %}
{{ example(markup, "Responsive equal tracks") }}
<table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Minimum viewport width</th><th>Tracks</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>grid-md-1fr-4fr</code></td><td>820px</td><td><code>1fr 4fr</code> (existing behavior)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>grid-md-1fr-1fr</code></td><td>820px</td><td>Two equal tracks</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>grid-lg-1fr-2fr</code></td><td>960px</td><td>One share beside two shares</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>grid-lg-1fr-1fr-1fr</code></td><td>960px</td><td>Three equal tracks</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>New patterns use <code>minmax(0, 1fr)</code> (or <code>minmax(0, 2fr)</code>) so tracks can shrink without imposing a fixed minimum width. Breakpoints follow the viewport, so allow room for the tracks inside your actual container. See the <a href="/ui/layouts/">featured lesson and supporting grid</a> for nested one-to-two and three-column patterns.</p>
<h3 id="customize-track-patterns">Customize selected patterns</h3>
<p>Set <code>$grid-patterns</code> before importing the library, in <code>src/my-epic-css/_custom.scss</code>. The outer keys select existing breakpoints; inner keys become class suffixes, and values supply the track list. Only listed combinations are generated. This replaces the default map rather than merging it; keep any defaults your templates use.</p>
<pre><code>$grid-patterns: (
  md: (
    "1fr-4fr": (1fr 4fr),
    "1fr-1fr": (minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr)),
  ),
  lg: (
    "1fr-2fr": (minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 2fr)),
    "1fr-1fr-1fr": (minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr)),
  ),
);</code></pre>
<h3 id="add-track-pattern">Add a track pattern when needed</h3>
<p>Three, four, five, or mixed-proportion tracks use the same generator. For four equal columns, insert this entry into your existing <code>$grid-patterns</code> map in <code>_custom.scss</code>, alongside the <code>md</code> and <code>lg</code> entries above:</p>
<pre><code>xl: (
  "1fr-1fr-1fr-1fr": (minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr)),
),</code></pre>
<p>The outer key <code>xl</code> selects 1200px. The inner key supplies the suffix for <code>grid-xl-1fr-1fr-1fr-1fr</code>; its track list becomes <code>grid-template-columns</code>. Names do not calculate the tracks: keep the key descriptive of its values. This is an illustrative configuration, not an included default. The class exists only after you configure it and recompile Sass.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;div class="grid grid-xl-1fr-1fr-1fr-1fr gap-24"&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;First idea&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;Second idea&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;Third idea&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;Fourth idea&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
<p><code>grid</code> enables display and <code>gap-24</code> separates the tracks. With this markup, items occupy one implicit column below 1200px and four columns from 1200px. Add a different selected pattern to the map when you need five tracks or mixed proportions; unconfigured combinations are not generated.</p>
<p>For local development, run <code>npm start</code> in the consumer project to compile Sass and start the watchers. For the documentation site's filtered build, run <code>npm run build</code>. For the starter theme, run <code>npm run epic-build</code>, then <code>npm run epic-light</code>, then <code>npm run build-production</code>, allowing each command to finish. Stop development watchers before a filtered production build.</p>
<p>The separate <code>$sidebar-widths</code> map continues to generate fixed-width sidebar layouts. The former auto-fitting column helpers have been removed; choose explicit equal tracks for equal-column layouts.</p>
<h2 id="gap">Space between tracks</h2>
<p><code>gap-16</code> sets both row and column gap to one rem. These are the current spacer keys:</p>
<p><code>0, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 16, 18, 20, 24, 32, 40, 56, 60, 64, 80, 100</code>.</p>
<p>The values are rem-based Sass multipliers, not a general pixel parser. In the current scale both <code>gap-16</code> and <code>gap-18</code> resolve to one rem.</p>
<h2 id="sidebar">Sidebar layouts</h2>
<p>Combine <code>grid</code>, a sidebar utility, and a gap utility. Both sidebars use one <code>minmax(0, 1fr)</code> track below the <code>lg</code> breakpoint (960 pixels), then a fixed-width sidebar beside flexible main content. The breakpoint follows the viewport; allow enough container space for both tracks and the gap.</p>
<h3 id="left-sidebar">Fixed-width left sidebar</h3>
<p>Put the sidebar first. The docs use <code>grid grid-sidebar-left-lg-208px gap-32</code>: a 208-pixel left track from 960 pixels, with a two-rem gap.</p>
{% set markup %}<div class="grid grid-sidebar-left-lg-208px gap-32">
  <div class="docs-demo-item">Sidebar</div>
  <div class="docs-demo-item">Main content</div>
</div>{% endset %}
{{ example(markup, "Responsive left sidebar grid") }}
<p>Below 960 pixels, the sidebar and main content stack in HTML order. Put the sidebar first to keep it above the main content. To stack it below while keeping it on the left at 960 pixels and up, put the main content first in the HTML and add <code>order-lg-last</code> to that direct grid child. Reading and keyboard navigation still follow HTML order.</p>
<h3 id="right-sidebar">Fixed-width right sidebar</h3>
<p>Put main content first. Use <code>grid grid-sidebar-right-lg-344px gap-40</code> for a 344-pixel right track from 960 pixels, with a 2.5rem gap. See the <a href="/ui/layouts/">posts and right-sidebar example</a>.</p>
{% set markup %}<div class="grid grid-sidebar-right-lg-344px gap-40">
  <div class="docs-demo-item">Main content</div>
  <div class="docs-demo-item">Sidebar</div>
</div>{% endset %}
{{ example(markup, "Responsive right sidebar grid") }}
<table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Tracks from 960 pixels</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>grid-sidebar-left-lg-208px</code></td><td><code>208px minmax(0, 1fr)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>grid-sidebar-right-lg-344px</code></td><td><code>minmax(0, 1fr) 344px</code></td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3 id="customize-sidebar-width">Customize sidebar widths</h3>
<p>Set <code>$sidebar-widths</code> near the top of <code>src/my-epic-css/_custom.scss</code>, then rebuild Sass. The fallback map in <code>_variables.scss</code> uses <code>!default</code>, preserving a map assigned before the library is imported.</p>
<pre><code>$sidebar-widths: (
  narrow: 208px,
  wide: 344px,
);</code></pre>
<p>Each map value generates both left and right utilities: <code>grid-sidebar-left-lg-208px</code>, <code>grid-sidebar-right-lg-208px</code>, <code>grid-sidebar-left-lg-344px</code>, and <code>grid-sidebar-right-lg-344px</code>. Keep the narrow sidebar for navigation and use the wider left sidebar for author profiles.</p>
<p>Map keys label the settings; widths and units form the class names. Add <code>medium: 280px</code> to generate both <code>grid-sidebar-left-lg-280px</code> and <code>grid-sidebar-right-lg-280px</code>. Update HTML when changing an existing width. The map replaces the individual left/right width settings.</p>
<p>Replace the former fixed right-sidebar and percentage left-sidebar utilities with the configurable utilities and a separate gap utility. The former small-sidebar helper is removed. The fractional helper is now <code>grid-md-1fr-4fr</code>: combine it with <code>grid</code> for <code>1fr 4fr</code> tracks from 820 pixels.</p>
<h2 id="containers">Containers and widths</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>wrapper</code></td><td>Five percent padding on each side.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>container</code></td><td>Centered container with the Sass-configured maximum width: 1040 pixels on this site, with a 1000-pixel library fallback. See <a href="/docs/wrapper-and-container/#customize-container-width">container customization</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>container-post</code></td><td>Centered container with an independently configured <code>$container-post-width</code>: 1000-pixel fallback and a 900-pixel custom setting. See <a href="/docs/wrapper-and-container/#customize-post-container">post container customization</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>w-100</code></td><td>Full available width.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>margin-auto</code></td><td><code>margin: 0 auto</code>, resetting vertical margins and centering horizontally where the available width permits.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>The retired breakpoint percentage-width helpers are no longer generated. Use fractional grid tracks to share available space after gaps, or an explicit inline <code>max-width</code> to cap a reading region while allowing it to fill narrower screens.</p>
